            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of John Calvin</strong><br>\nJohn Calvin was born on July 10, 1509, in Noyon, Picardy, France. He was the first of four sons who survived infancy. His mother, Jeanne le Franc, was the daughter of an innkeeper, and his father, Gérard Cauvin, was a church notary who later worked as a fiscal agent for the Count of Noyon. Calvin was raised in a strict Roman Catholic family, and his father intended him to become a priest. He received a thorough education at the Colleges of La Marche and Montaigu in Paris, focusing on the humanities and theology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Education and Early Work</strong><br>\nAs a young man, Calvin studied in Paris, where he was exposed to Renaissance humanism, a movement that influenced him deeply. He originally pursued a career in theology, but his father redirected him to study law at the University of Orleans. He became proficient in Latin and developed an interest in classical literature and the writings of early church fathers. After his father's death, Calvin returned to Paris, where he began to question the teachings of the Catholic Church and embrace the principles of the Reformation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Conversion and Exile</strong><br>\nAround 1533, Calvin experienced a religious conversion. Although he never described it in detail, it led him to break away from the Roman Catholic Church. Following a violent upheaval against Protestants in France, Calvin decided to leave his home country. He became a wandering scholar, moving between Basel, Ferrara, and finally Geneva. His writings began to circulate, and he became known for his criticism of the Catholic Church and his support for the Protestant cause.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
