            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Writing 'Institutes of the Christian Religion'</strong><br>\nIn 1536, Calvin published the first edition of his seminal work \"Institutes of the Christian Religion,\" which laid out the tenets of his religious beliefs and the principles of the Protestant faith. The work was revolutionary because it systematically presented the doctrines of Protestantism, contrasting them with the Roman Catholic practices of the time. \"Institutes\" was revised and expanded throughout Calvin's life, becoming a key text for Reformed churches worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Role in Geneva</strong><br>\nCalvin was invited to settle in Geneva by William Farel, a Protestant minister who saw in Calvin a strong ally. Initially, Calvin intended to stay only a night, but Farel convinced him to help reform the city's church. Their efforts were not without controversy, and both men were temporarily expelled from Geneva due to disputes with the city's authorities. However, Calvin's vision for a community based on his Christian ideals eventually took hold, and he returned to Geneva, where he played a significant role in shaping its religious life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Establishing the Calvinist Theology</strong><br>\nCalvin's work in Geneva led to the establishment of a distinctive Christian theology known as Calvinism. It emphasizes the sovereignty of God, the doctrine of predestination, and the absolute depravity of man. Calvin's ideas influenced various aspects of society, including the economy, education, and governance. Under his guidance, Geneva became the center of Protestant reform, attracting believers from all over Europe who sought refuge from religious persecution.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
