          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Education and Government</strong><br>\nCalvin believed in the importance of education for promoting the Reformed faith and implemented a system of schools in Geneva that would teach both the basics and advanced subjects. He also set up the Geneva Academy, which became a training ground for Protestant leaders. Calvin's teachings on government emphasized the role of the church in state affairs and promoted the idea that civil authorities should be subordinate to the divine laws as interpreted by the church.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Opposition and Controversies</strong><br>\nCalvin's reforms met with resistance and controversy. He was often in conflict with opposing religious groups and sometimes with the civic authorities in Geneva. His involvement in the trial and execution of Michael Servetus for heresy in 1553 is one of the most notable instances of his contentious legacy. This event has been widely debated, with some viewing Calvin as a defender of orthodox Christianity and others as a proponent of religious intolerance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nJohn Calvin passed away on May 27, 1564, in Geneva. His impact on the Reformation and on the establishment of a Protestant ethic was profound. Calvin's theological work and organizational skills led to the widespread adoption of his reform ideas after his death. Today, Calvin is remembered as a central figure in the history of the Reformation, and his theological views continue to be influential in Christian communities that identify with the Reformed tradition.</p>\n"
              },
